% ============================================================================
% band-secondary-use — for the EM bands where NASA names a SECOND everyday
% use in the same sentence, that second use (adj-facts-stdlib, PHYSICS).
% ============================================================================
% A sibling to the already-shipped `em-spectrum.adj` (`band_use(band,
% application)`, ONE representative everyday use per EM band): a new
% `band_secondary_use(band, application)` table names a SECOND everyday use
% the SAME already-cited NASA "Imagine the Universe!" page states for a band,
% decoded from text already sitting unused inside `em-spectrum.adj`'s own
% provenance block — no new WebFetch. That table's `application` column has
% room for only the FIRST everyday use; the SAME already-quoted NASA
% sentences also name a second, distinct use for two of the seven bands that
% never made it into a row:
%
%   microwave → astronomy
%     "Microwave radiation will cook your popcorn in just a few minutes, but
%      is also used by astronomers to learn about the structure of nearby
%      galaxies."
%   x_ray → airport_security
%     "A dentist uses X-rays to image your teeth, and airport security uses
%      them to see through your bag."
%
% Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? band_secondary_use(microwave, $Application)      % astronomy
%
% This is a native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5, a TWO-column table like
% `em-spectrum.adj`'s own `band_use`): each `row` lowers to a ground relation
% `band_secondary_use(band, application)` carrying the citation, so the
% lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query — the engine returns the
% secondary use WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero model calls, and
% abstains on the other five bands (radio, infrared, visible, ultraviolet,
% gamma_ray), whose own already-cited spans in `em-spectrum.adj` name only
% ONE use each, with no second use to decode here.
%
% Only TWO of `em-spectrum.adj`'s seven bands have a genuinely second,
% distinct everyday use in the ALREADY-cited spans; this table is
% deliberately narrow rather than inventing a use the source does not state.
% The query also runs BACKWARD — bind a use and recall which band it is an
% instance of:
%
%     ? band_secondary_use($Band, airport_security)   % x_ray
%
% Provenance: reproduces, byte-for-byte, the SAME microwave span already
% quoted inside `em-spectrum.adj`'s own provenance block, from the SAME NASA
% Goddard Space Flight Center "Imagine the Universe!" education page that
% table already cites — no new WebFetch, only a different schema decoding a
% part of each already-verified quote. An ADJ `table` carries ONE provenance
% envelope (per `em-spectrum.adj`'s own convention), so `source`/`locator`/
% `trust` below hold the microwave span; the x_ray row's own span is listed
% above for independent auditability. `trust authoritative` — the same tier
% `em-spectrum.adj` already carries (a primary U.S. government NASA
% education page).
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table band_secondary_use {
    columns band, application

    row (microwave, astronomy)
    row (x_ray, airport_security)

    source "Microwave radiation will cook your popcorn in just a few minutes, but is also used by astronomers to learn about the structure of nearby galaxies."
    locator "https://imagine.gsfc.nasa.gov/science/toolbox/emspectrum1.html"
    trust authoritative
}
